---

---

<dialog
  id="faq-dialog"
  class="w-full max-w-4xl border-0 bg-transparent p-4 outline-none backdrop:bg-gray-900/50 dark:backdrop:bg-gray-900/80"
>
  <div
    class="relative max-h-[85vh] overflow-y-auto rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6 text-gray-900 shadow-2xl dark:border-gray-800 dark:bg-gray-900 dark:text-white md:p-8"
  >
    <!-- Close Button -->
    <button
      onclick="this.closest('dialog').close()"
      class="absolute right-4 top-4 text-gray-400 transition duration-200 hover:text-gray-600 dark:hover:text-white"
      aria-label="Close FAQ dialog"
    >
      <svg
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        width="24"
        height="24"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
        class="h-6 w-6"
        ><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line><line
          x1="6"
          y1="6"
          x2="18"
          y2="18"></line></svg
      >
    </button>

    <h2
      class="mb-8 text-center text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-4xl"
    >
      Frequently Asked Questions & Installation Guide
    </h2>

    <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div
        class="rounded-xl border border-gray-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm dark:border-gray-800 dark:bg-gray-800"
      >
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
          What is rEFInd?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          rEFInd is an advanced, open-source UEFI boot manager maintained by <a
            href="https://www.rodsbooks.com/refind/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="font-semibold text-dracula-400 hover:underline">Rod Smith</a
          >. Designed for systems that dual-boot or multi-boot multiple
          operating systems—such as Linux distributions (Ubuntu, Arch, Fedora),
          macOS, and Windows—rEFInd offers a clean, visual alternative to
          default text-only bootloaders like GRUB.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="rounded-xl border border-gray-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm dark:border-gray-800 dark:bg-gray-800"
      >
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
          Where is the refind.conf configuration file located?
        </h3>
        <p
          class="mb-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"
        >
          The location of the <code>refind.conf</code> file depends on your operating
          system and setup:
        </p>
        <ul
          class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-xs text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"
        >
          <li>
            <strong>Linux:</strong> Typically located at <code
              >/boot/efi/EFI/refind/refind.conf</code
            > or <code>/boot/EFI/refind/refind.conf</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>macOS:</strong> Found at <code
              >/Volumes/ESP/EFI/refind/refind.conf</code
            > (requires mounting the EFI System Partition first).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Windows:</strong> Located at <code
              >S:\EFI\refind\refind.conf</code
            > (after mounting the ESP to drive S: as Administrator).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div
        class="col-span-full rounded-xl border border-gray-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm dark:border-gray-800 dark:bg-gray-800"
      >
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
          How do I install a rEFInd theme?
        </h3>
        <div
          class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"
        >
          <p>
            Follow these 4 simple steps to install and apply any of the 140+
            themes from our collection:
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5 text-xs md:text-sm">
            <li>
              <strong>Locate the themes directory:</strong> Find the <code
                >themes/</code
              > folder within your rEFInd installation directory. If it doesn't exist,
              create it:
              <pre
                class="mt-1 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-gray-50 p-2 font-mono text-xs text-dracula-400 dark:bg-gray-900">mkdir -p /boot/efi/EFI/refind/themes</pre>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Download the theme:</strong> Clone the GitHub repository of
              your chosen theme directly into the themes directory, or download and
              extract the ZIP archive:
              <pre
                class="mt-1 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-gray-50 p-2 font-mono text-xs text-dracula-400 dark:bg-gray-900">git clone https://github.com/user/theme-repo.git /boot/efi/EFI/refind/themes/theme-name</pre>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Modify the configuration file:</strong> Open your <code
                >refind.conf</code
              > file in a text editor with administrative privileges, and add the
              following line at the absolute bottom:
              <pre
                class="mt-1 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-gray-50 p-2 font-mono text-xs text-dracula-400 dark:bg-gray-900">include themes/theme-name/theme.conf</pre>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Verify and Reboot:</strong> Save the file. When you reboot
              your computer, rEFInd will render the new interface theme.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="col-span-full rounded-xl border border-gray-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm dark:border-gray-800 dark:bg-gray-800"
      >
        <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
          Can I customize OS icons and backgrounds?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          Yes! rEFInd themes define custom graphics for operating system
          selection icons (Linux, Windows, macOS, Arch, Ubuntu, etc.), tool
          icons (reboot, shutdown, firmware), selection highlights, and banner
          backgrounds. You can easily swap or customize individual <code
            >.png</code
          > icon files inside the theme's directory to perfectly suit your preferences.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</dialog>

<script is:inline>
  // Close dialog on clicking backdrop
  const dialog = document.getElementById("faq-dialog");
  dialog?.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    if (event.target === dialog) {
      dialog.close();
    }
  });
</script>
